      {
        "id": "def-qft-regulated-polynomial-hamiltonian",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Finite-lattice polynomial Hamiltonian data",
        "reference": "items/def-qft-regulated-polynomial-hamiltonian.md#Definition",
        "design_state": "Authored draft with complete local argument; independent review remains pending.",
        "dependency_level": 0,
        "deps": [
          "lem-l-two-with-the-integral-pairing-is-a-hilbert-space",
          "def-countable-choice"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "lem-l-two-with-the-integral-pairing-is-a-hilbert-space": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Assume Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). A **finite-mode regulator** in this item is a fixed\nfinite nonempty label set $J$ with $N:=|J|\\ge1$, a fixed enumeration of $J$ by\n$\\{1,\\ldots,N\\}$, and the real coordinate vector\n$q=(q_1,\\ldots,q_N)\\in\\mathbb R^N$. The labels may name sites of a finite\nlattice or finitely many modes; their geometry enters only through the chosen\npolynomial below. No limit in $N$ is part of this definition.\n\nChoose real coefficients $a_1,\\ldots,a_N>0$, constants $c>0$ and $C_0\\ge0$,\nand a real polynomial $V\\in\\mathbb R[q_1,\\ldots,q_N]$ such that\n$$V(q)\\ge c|q|^2-C_0\\qquad(q\\in\\mathbb R^N).$$\nLet $H_N:=L^2(\\mathbb R^N,dq;\\mathbb C)$, with vectors identified up to almost\neverywhere equality and inner product\n$$\\langle u,v\\rangle:=\\int_{\\mathbb R^N}u(q)\\overline{v(q)}\\,dq,$$\nlinear in the first variable. Under Countable Choice this is a complex Hilbert\nspace by [[lem-l-two-with-the-integral-pairing-is-a-hilbert-space]]. Let\n$D_0:=C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^N;\\mathbb C)$, the smooth compactly supported\ncomplex-valued functions. The associated **polynomial differential expression**\nis the map\n$$h_0:D_0\\to H_N,\\qquad h_0u:=-\\sum_{j=1}^Na_j\\partial_j^2u+Vu.$$\nIt is well defined because each derivative of $u$ and the product $Vu$ are\nsmooth and compactly supported. Set $W:=V+C_0+1$, so\n$W(q)\\ge1+c|q|^2$. This definition specifies finite mathematical data and a\ndifferential expression; it does not choose a self-adjoint operator domain or\nassert a continuum or infinite-volume limit.",
        "strategy": "Definition; no proof supplied.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Gerald Teschl, Mathematical Methods in Quantum Mechanics, 2nd ed., §§2.3 and 8.3, pp.76–83 and 212–214",
              "url": "https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~gerald/ftp/book-schroe/schroe2.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "Richard S. Laugesen, Spectral Theory of Partial Differential Equations — Lecture Notes, Chapter 8, pp.48–50",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1203.2344v1"
            }
          ]
        },
        "library": "mathematics"
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-qft-coercive-polynomial-form-domain",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Coercive polynomial form completion and injection",
        "reference": "items/lem-qft-coercive-polynomial-form-domain.md#Statement",
        "design_state": "Authored draft with complete local argument; independent review remains pending.",
        "dependency_level": 1,
        "deps": [
          "def-qft-regulated-polynomial-hamiltonian",
          "lem-l-two-with-the-integral-pairing-is-a-hilbert-space",
          "thm-c-c-infinity-rn-is-dense-in-l-p-of-rn",
          "thm-cauchy-schwarz-in-an-inner-product-space",
          "def-countable-choice"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-qft-regulated-polynomial-hamiltonian": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-l-two-with-the-integral-pairing-is-a-hilbert-space": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-c-c-infinity-rn-is-dense-in-l-p-of-rn": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-cauchy-schwarz-in-an-inner-product-space": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Assume Countable Choice. Use the finite data and notation of\n[[def-qft-regulated-polynomial-hamiltonian]]: $N\\ge1$,\n$H_N=L^2(\\mathbb R^N,dq;\\mathbb C)$, $D_0=C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^N;\\mathbb C)$,\nand $W=V+C_0+1\\ge1+c|q|^2$. For $u,v\\in D_0$ define\n$$a(u,v):=\\sum_{j=1}^N a_j\\int_{\\mathbb R^N}(\\partial_j u)\\overline{\\partial_jv}\\,dq+\\int_{\\mathbb R^N}(W+1)u\\overline v\\,dq,$$\nand $\\|u\\|_Q:=a(u,u)^{1/2}$. Let $Q$ be the completion of this inner-product\nspace, constructed as equivalence classes of $\\|\\cdot\\|_Q$-Cauchy sequences.\nThen $Q$ is a complex Hilbert space and the map sending a Cauchy class to its\n$L^2$ limit is a continuous injective linear map $Q\\hookrightarrow H_N$ with\ndense image. Each $u\\in Q$ has weak derivatives $\\partial_j u\\in L^2$ and a\nweighted function $\\sqrt{W+1}\\,u\\in L^2$, and\n$$\\|u\\|_Q^2=\\sum_{j=1}^N a_j\\|\\partial_j u\\|_2^2+\\|\\sqrt{W+1}\\,u\\|_2^2.$$\nHere $g_j=\\partial_j u$ means explicitly that\n$\\int u\\,\\overline{\\partial_j\\varphi}=-\\int g_j\\overline\\varphi$ for every\n$\\varphi\\in D_0$.",
        "strategy": "**Proof technique:** direct.\n\n1.1 Since $W+1\\ge2$ and every $a_j>0$, $a$ is a Hermitian positive-definite sesquilinear form on $D_0$: in particular $a(u,u)=\\sum_j a_j\\|\\partial_j u\\|_2^2+\\|\\sqrt{W+1}\\,u\\|_2^2\\ge2\\|u\\|_2^2$, and $a(u,u)=0$ forces $u=0$ in $H_N$. Thus its Cauchy-sequence completion with the stated equivalence relation is a well-defined inner-product space. [F1, F2, algebra]\n\n2.1 To see that the completion is complete, let $(X_n)$ be a Cauchy sequence of its classes. By [A1], choose $u_n\\in D_0$ with $\\|[u_n]-X_n\\|_Q<2^{-n}$ for each $n$. The triangle inequality makes $(u_n)$ $Q$-Cauchy; its class $X$ is defined by the Cauchy-sequence construction, and $\\|X_n-X\\|_Q\\le2^{-n}+\\|[u_n]-X\\|_Q\\to0$. Hence $Q$ is complete and therefore Hilbert. [A1, step 1.1, algebra]\n\n2.2 For a $Q$-Cauchy representative sequence $(u_n)$, the inequalities $\\|u_n-u_m\\|_2\\le\\|u_n-u_m\\|_Q$, $\\sqrt{a_j}\\|\\partial_j u_n-\\partial_j u_m\\|_2\\le\\|u_n-u_m\\|_Q$, and $\\|\\sqrt{W+1}(u_n-u_m)\\|_2\\le\\|u_n-u_m\\|_Q$ show that $u_n$, each $\\partial_j u_n$, and $\\sqrt{W+1}u_n$ converge in $L^2$ by [F2]; write their limits as $u$, $g_j$, and $h$. The first limit defines a continuous map $Q\\to H_N$, since $\\|u\\|_2\\le\\|[u_n]\\|_Q$, and equivalent Cauchy sequences have the same $L^2$ limit. [F1, F2, step 1.1]\n\n3.1 For every $\\varphi\\in D_0$, integration by parts gives $\\int u_n\\overline{\\partial_j\\varphi}=-\\int (\\partial_j u_n)\\overline\\varphi$. Passing to the limit in both integrals by $L^2$ convergence and Cauchy–Schwarz yields $\\int u\\overline{\\partial_j\\varphi}=-\\int g_j\\overline\\varphi$, so $g_j$ is the stated weak derivative. [step 2.2, F2, F3, algebra]\n\n3.2 On each compact ball $B_R$, the continuous polynomial $W+1$ is bounded. Therefore $\\sqrt{W+1}u_n\\to\\sqrt{W+1}u$ in $L^2(B_R)$, while the same sequence converges there to $h$; uniqueness of limits gives $h=\\sqrt{W+1}u$ almost everywhere on every integer-radius ball and hence almost everywhere on $\\mathbb R^N$. Taking limits of the component norms in $a(u_n,u_n)$ now gives the displayed formula for $\\|u\\|_Q^2$. [step 2.2, F1, F2, algebra]\n\n4.1 If $u=0$ in $L^2$, then step 3.1 says $\\langle g_j,\\varphi\\rangle=0$ for every $\\varphi\\in D_0$; this pairing is continuous by [F3] and $D_0$ is dense by [F4], so it vanishes on all of $H_N$, and taking $\\varphi=g_j$ gives $g_j=0$. Step 3.2 then gives $h=0$; the norm formula yields $\\|[u_n]\\|_Q=0$, so the embedding is injective. Finally its image contains $D_0$, which is dense in $H_N$ by [F4]; thus the continuous injection has dense image and all claims follow. [step 3.1, step 3.2, F3, F4, algebra] ∎",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Gerald Teschl, Mathematical Methods in Quantum Mechanics, 2nd ed., §2.3, pp.76–83",
              "url": "https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~gerald/ftp/book-schroe/schroe2.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "Richard S. Laugesen, Spectral Theory of Partial Differential Equations — Lecture Notes, Chapters 4 and 8, pp.27–32 and 48–50",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1203.2344v1"
            }
          ]
        },
        "library": "mathematics",
        "proof_strategy": "direct"
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-qft-cube-poincare-compact-form-inclusion",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Cube mean estimate and compact form inclusion",
        "reference": "items/lem-qft-cube-poincare-compact-form-inclusion.md#Statement",
        "design_state": "Authored draft with complete local argument; independent review remains pending.",
        "dependency_level": 2,
        "deps": [
          "lem-qft-coercive-polynomial-form-domain",
          "def-compact-linear-operator",
          "thm-cauchy-schwarz-in-an-inner-product-space",
          "thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "lem-qft-coercive-polynomial-form-domain": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-compact-linear-operator": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-cauchy-schwarz-in-an-inner-product-space": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Assume the finite-dimensional coercive form data and Hilbert space $Q$ of\n[[lem-qft-coercive-polynomial-form-domain]]. The inclusion\n$J:Q\\hookrightarrow H_N$ is compact: the closure in $H_N$ of the image of\nevery bounded subset of $Q$ is compact in the metric topology. The proof below\nuses an explicit cube-mean approximation for each finite $N$ and does not\nassume a general Rellich theorem.",
        "strategy": "**Proof technique:** direct.\n\n**Given:** Countable Choice as inherited through [F1], the finite data, and an arbitrary $Q$-bounded set.\n\n1.1 Let $C$ be a cube of side $\\delta>0$ and $u\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^N;\\mathbb C)$, with $u_C:=|C|^{-1}\\int_Cu$. Expanding the square and using Fubini on the finite cube gives\n$$\\int_C|u-u_C|^2=\\frac1{2|C|}\\int_C\\int_C|u(x)-u(y)|^2\\,dx\\,dy.$$\nTelescope $u(x)-u(y)$ through the $N$ coordinate changes and use $|\\sum_{j=1}^Nz_j|^2\\le N\\sum_j|z_j|^2$. For the $j$th change, the fundamental theorem of calculus and Cauchy–Schwarz give $|D_j(x,y)|^2\\le\\delta\\int_{\\min(x_j,y_j)}^{\\max(x_j,y_j)}|\\partial_ju|^2$. For fixed values of the other coordinates and fixed $s\\in[0,\\delta]$, the measure of pairs $(x_j,y_j)$ whose segment contains $s$ is $2s(\\delta-s)\\le\\delta^2/2$; integrating the displayed bound over the cube variables and dividing by $2|C|$ therefore yields the safe estimate\n$$\\|u-u_C\\|_{L^2(C)}^2\\le N\\delta^2\\sum_{j=1}^N\\|\\partial_j u\\|_{L^2(C)}^2.$$\nIt applies to complex $u$ because the squared-modulus Cauchy–Schwarz inequality is used. [F3, F4, algebra]\n\n1.2 For $R>0$ and $u\\in Q$, the coercive weight gives\n$$\\int_{|q|>R}|u(q)|^2\\,dq\\le\\frac1{cR^2}\\int_{|q|>R}c|q|^2|u(q)|^2\\,dq\\le\\frac1{cR^2}\\|u\\|_Q^2.$$\nThus the $L^2$ tails of the $Q$ unit ball are uniformly small for large $R$. [F1, algebra]\n\n2.1 The estimate of step 1.1 passes to every $u\\in Q$: approximate $u$ in the $Q$ norm by elements of $C_c^\\infty$ (the defining completion), restrict to $C$, and use [F1] to pass the $L^2$ and derivative limits. [step 1.1, F1]\n\n3.1 Fix $\\varepsilon>0$. Choose $R$ so the squared tail in step 1.2 is below $\\varepsilon^2/16$. For the $Q$ unit ball, [F1] gives $\\sum_j\\|\\partial_j u\\|_2^2\\le\\sum_j a_j^{-1}$. Choose an integer $M$ large enough that $\\delta:=2R/M$ satisfies $N\\delta^2\\sum_j a_j^{-1}<\\varepsilon^2/16$, and partition $[-R,R]^N$ into the resulting finitely many cubes of side $\\delta$. Summing the squared estimates of step 2.1 over these disjoint cubes bounds the total mean-approximation error by $N\\delta^2\\sum_j\\|\\partial_j u\\|_2^2<\\varepsilon^2/16$. The squared error outside $[-R,R]^N$ is the tail from step 1.2; thus replacing $u$ on each cube by its mean and setting it to zero outside the large cube has total squared $L^2$ error below $\\varepsilon^2/8$, hence norm error below $\\varepsilon/2$. [step 2.1, step 1.2, F1, choose, algebra]\n\n4.1 For the finite partition in step 3.1, the piecewise-constant mean function $m_u$ satisfies $\\|m_u\\|_2^2=\\sum_C|C||u_C|^2\\le\\sum_C\\|u\\|_{L^2(C)}^2\\le\\|u\\|_2^2\\le1$ by Cauchy--Schwarz [F3]. Thus each mean coordinate has modulus at most $|C|^{-1/2}$, so the vectors of means lie in a bounded box in a finite-dimensional complex coordinate space. Subdivide its real-and-imaginary coordinates into finitely many boxes of sufficiently small side length so that the associated piecewise-constant functions approximate every mean vector to $L^2$ error below $\\varepsilon/2$. Combining with step 3.1 gives a finite $\\varepsilon$-net for the image of the $Q$ unit ball. Scaling shows that the image of every bounded $Q$ set is totally bounded. [step 3.1, F1, F3, algebra]\n\n5.1 Let $K$ be the $H_N$ closure of the image of the $Q$ unit ball. It is complete because it is closed in the complete space $H_N$ [F1]. Suppose an open cover of $K$ has no finite subcover. For each $n\\ge1$, step 4.1 supplies a finite $2^{-n-2}$-net for the image of the $Q$ unit ball with centers in $H_N$; the corresponding closed balls of radius $2^{-n-1}$ cover its closure $K$. Starting with $C_0=K$, choose the least ball index for which $C_n:=C_{n-1}\\cap\\overline B(p_n,2^{-n-1})$ has no finite subcover. Such an index exists, since otherwise finitely many finite subcovers would cover $C_{n-1}$. Each $C_n$ is nonempty, closed, nested, and has diameter at most $2^{-n}$. For $m>n$, any point of nonempty $C_m$ lies in both selected balls, so $\\|p_m-p_n\\|_2\\le2^{-m-1}+2^{-n-1}$; hence $(p_n)$ is Cauchy and converges to some $p\\in H_N$. Since $C_n$ is nonempty and is contained in both $K$ and $\\overline B(p_n,2^{-n-1})$, the distance from $p_n$ to $K$ is at most $2^{-n-1}$. Therefore $\\operatorname{dist}(p,K)\\le\\|p-p_n\\|_2+2^{-n-1}\\to0$; closedness of $K$ gives $p\\in K$. For fixed $n$ and every $m>n$, nonempty $C_m\\subseteq C_n$ has a point $y\\in C_m$ with $\\|p_m-y\\|_2\\le2^{-m-1}$. Thus there are points of $C_n$ arbitrarily close to $p$, and closedness gives $p\\in C_n$. An open set of the cover containing $p$ contains $K\\cap B(p,r)$ for some $r>0$; for large $n$, $\\operatorname{diam}(C_n)<r$, so that one open set covers $C_n$, a contradiction. Thus $K$ is compact. All recursive ball choices are least admissible indices in finite lists. [step 4.1, F1, algebra]\n\n6.1 Step 5.1 proves that the closed-unit-ball image under $J$ has compact closure; applying the equivalent criterion in [F2] to the linear inclusion proves that $J:Q\\hookrightarrow H_N$ is compact. [step 5.1, F2] ∎",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Gerald Teschl, Mathematical Methods in Quantum Mechanics, 2nd ed., §2.3, pp.76–83",
              "url": "https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~gerald/ftp/book-schroe/schroe2.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "Richard S. Laugesen, Spectral Theory of Partial Differential Equations — Lecture Notes, Chapter 8, pp.48–50",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1203.2344v1"
            }
          ]
        },
        "library": "mathematics",
        "proof_strategy": "direct"
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-qft-hermite-oscillator-research-supplier",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Anisotropic Hermite oscillator and quadratic form basis",
        "reference": "items/lem-qft-hermite-oscillator-research-supplier.md#Statement",
        "design_state": "Authored draft with complete local argument; independent review remains pending.",
        "dependency_level": 2,
        "deps": [
          "lem-qft-coercive-polynomial-form-domain",
          "thm-nrqm-oscillator-multidimensional-oscillator-completeness",
          "thm-nrqm-oscillator-hermite-recurrence",
          "thm-nrqm-oscillator-oscillator-scaling-and-exact-realization",
          "thm-hilbert-space-fourier-expansion",
          "thm-cauchy-schwarz-in-an-inner-product-space",
          "def-countable-choice"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "lem-qft-coercive-polynomial-form-domain": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-nrqm-oscillator-multidimensional-oscillator-completeness": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-nrqm-oscillator-hermite-recurrence": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-nrqm-oscillator-oscillator-scaling-and-exact-realization": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-hilbert-space-fourier-expansion": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-cauchy-schwarz-in-an-inner-product-space": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $N\\ge1$, $a_1,\\ldots,a_N>0$ and $c>0$. Let\n$Q_{\\mathrm{osc}}$ be the completion of $C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^N;\\mathbb C)$\nfor the form inner product\n$$a_{\\mathrm{osc}}(u,v):=\\sum_{j=1}^N a_j\\int (\\partial_j u)\\overline{\\partial_jv} +\\int (c|q|^2+2)u\\overline v.$$\nThis is the space $Q$ of\n[[lem-qft-coercive-polynomial-form-domain]] for $V(q)=c|q|^2$ and $C_0=0$.\nFor $\\mathbf n=(n_1,\\ldots,n_N)\\in\\mathbb N_0^N$, there is a smooth product\nHermite function $\\psi_{\\mathbf n}$ such that\n$$\\langle\\psi_{\\mathbf n},\\psi_{\\mathbf m}\\rangle_{L^2}=\\delta_{\\mathbf n\\mathbf m},\\qquad \\overline{\\operatorname{span}}\\{\\psi_{\\mathbf n}\\}=L^2(\\mathbb R^N),$$\nand\n$$\\left(-\\sum_{j=1}^Na_j\\partial_j^2+c|q|^2\\right)\\psi_{\\mathbf n} =\\epsilon_{\\mathbf n}\\psi_{\\mathbf n},\\qquad \\epsilon_{\\mathbf n}:=\\sum_{j=1}^N(2n_j+1)\\sqrt{a_jc}.$$\nFor the ground multi-index $\\mathbf0$, the smooth representative is\n$$\\psi_{\\mathbf0}(q)=\\prod_{j=1}^N(b_j/\\pi)^{1/4}e^{-b_jq_j^2/2}, \\qquad b_j:=\\sqrt{c/a_j}.$$\nEach $\\psi_{\\mathbf n}$ belongs to $Q_{\\mathrm{osc}}$, and every $u\\in\nQ_{\\mathrm{osc}}$ satisfies the exact form expansion\n$$\\sum_{j=1}^N a_j\\|\\partial_j u\\|_2^2+ c\\||q|u\\|_2^2 =\\sum_{\\mathbf n\\in\\mathbb N_0^N}\\epsilon_{\\mathbf n} |\\langle u,\\psi_{\\mathbf n}\\rangle|^2.$$\nAll parameters are positive real mathematical parameters; this lemma makes no\nphysical identification.",
        "strategy": "**Proof technique:** direct.\n\n**Given:** the oscillator form, its completion, and positive coefficients $a_j,c$.\n\n1.1 In [F2] choose $\\hbar=1$, $m_j=(2a_j)^{-1}$ and $\\omega_j=2\\sqrt{a_jc}$. Then $\\hbar^2/(2m_j)=a_j$ and $m_j\\omega_j^2/2=c$. The eigenvalue from [F2] in coordinate $j$ is $\\omega_j(n_j+1/2)=(2n_j+1)\\sqrt{a_jc}$. Summing over the $N$ coordinates gives the displayed differential eigenvalue $\\epsilon_{\\mathbf n}$. In the same scaling, $b_j=m_j\\omega_j=\\sqrt{c/a_j}$ and the normalized $n_j=0$ Gaussian becomes $(b_j/\\pi)^{1/4}e^{-b_jq_j^2/2}$; taking the product gives the stated ground representative. [A1, F2, algebra]\n\n1.2 Each product Hermite function is a polynomial times a Gaussian, so it and all first derivatives have finite squared integrals after multiplication by any fixed polynomial. Choose a smooth radial cutoff $\\chi_R(q)=\\chi(q/R)$ with $\\chi=1$ on $|q|\\le1$, $\\chi=0$ on $|q|\\ge2$, and $|\\nabla\\chi_R|\\le C/R$. Such a $\\chi$ can be formed from $\\eta(s)=e^{-1/s}$ for $s>0$ and $\\eta(s)=0$ for $s\\le0$, by taking $\\chi(q)=\\eta(4-|q|^2)/(\\eta(4-|q|^2)+\\eta(|q|^2-1))$. Then $0\\le\\chi_R\\le1$ and $\\chi_R\\psi_{\\mathbf n}\\in C_c^\\infty$. The weighted-function error is supported on $|q|>R$ and bounded in squared integral by the integrable tail $(c|q|^2+2)|\\psi_{\\mathbf n}|^2$, so its $L^2$ norm tends to zero. For each derivative,\n$$\\partial_j((1-\\chi_R)\\psi_{\\mathbf n})=(1-\\chi_R)\\partial_j\\psi_{\\mathbf n}-(\\partial_j\\chi_R)\\psi_{\\mathbf n};$$\nthe first term tends to zero by its Gaussian tail and the second has norm at most $C\\|\\psi_{\\mathbf n}\\|_2/R$. Thus $\\chi_R\\psi_{\\mathbf n}$ converges in the form norm, proving $\\psi_{\\mathbf n}\\in Q_{\\mathrm{osc}}$. [F1, algebra]\n\n2.1 For $v\\in C_c^\\infty$, integration by parts and the eigenvalue identity in step 1.1 give\n$$a_{\\mathrm{osc}}(\\psi_{\\mathbf n},v)=(\\epsilon_{\\mathbf n}+2) \\langle\\psi_{\\mathbf n},v\\rangle.$$\nThe left side is continuous in the form norm of $v$ by Cauchy--Schwarz [F4] in $Q_{\\mathrm{osc}}$, and the right side is continuous since $\\|v\\|_2\\le\\|v\\|_{Q_{\\mathrm{osc}}}$. Density of $C_c^\\infty$ in its defining completion extends the identity to every $v\\in Q_{\\mathrm{osc}}$. Taking $v=\\psi_{\\mathbf m}$ proves that $\\xi_{\\mathbf n}:=(\\epsilon_{\\mathbf n}+2)^{-1/2}\\psi_{\\mathbf n}$ is orthonormal in $Q_{\\mathrm{osc}}$. If $u\\in Q_{\\mathrm{osc}}$ is orthogonal in this form inner product to every $\\xi_{\\mathbf n}$, conjugating the displayed identity gives $\\langle u,\\psi_{\\mathbf n}\\rangle=0$ for every $\\mathbf n$. Completeness in $L^2$ from [F2] makes the image of $u$ zero, and the injective inclusion in [F1] makes $u=0$ in $Q_{\\mathrm{osc}}$. Thus $(\\xi_{\\mathbf n})$ is complete there. [F1, F2, F4, step 1.1, algebra]\n\n3.1 Parseval in $Q_{\\mathrm{osc}}$ with the complete orthonormal family $(\\xi_{\\mathbf n})$ gives\n$$a_{\\mathrm{osc}}(u,u)=\\sum_{\\mathbf n}(\\epsilon_{\\mathbf n}+2) |\\langle u,\\psi_{\\mathbf n}\\rangle|^2.$$\nParseval in $L^2$ gives $\\|u\\|_2^2=\\sum_{\\mathbf n}|\\langle u,\\psi_{\\mathbf n}\\rangle|^2$. Subtracting $2\\|u\\|_2^2$ from the first identity yields the asserted expansion of the unshifted oscillator form. [F2, F3, step 2.1, algebra] ∎",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Gerald Teschl, Mathematical Methods in Quantum Mechanics, 2nd ed., §8.3, pp.212–214",
              "url": "https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~gerald/ftp/book-schroe/schroe2.pdf",
              "locator": "§8.3, printed pp.212–214: Hermite functions, ladder operators and the oscillator eigenbasis."
            },
            {
              "title": "Richard S. Laugesen, Spectral Theory of Partial Differential Equations — Lecture Notes, Chapter 3, pp.21–24",
