      {
        "id": "lem-qft-polynomial-positive-compact-resolvent",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Positive injective compact polynomial form resolvent",
        "reference": "items/lem-qft-polynomial-positive-compact-resolvent.md#Statement",
        "design_state": "Authored draft with complete local argument; independent review remains pending.",
        "dependency_level": 3,
        "deps": [
          "lem-qft-coercive-polynomial-form-domain",
          "lem-qft-cube-poincare-compact-form-inclusion",
          "thm-riesz-representation-for-hilbert-space",
          "thm-cauchy-schwarz-in-an-inner-product-space",
          "def-bounded-linear-operator",
          "def-compact-linear-operator",
          "def-self-adjoint-positive-unitary-and-normal-operator",
          "thm-double-orthogonal-complement-is-closure",
          "def-countable-choice"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "lem-qft-coercive-polynomial-form-domain": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-qft-cube-poincare-compact-form-inclusion": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-riesz-representation-for-hilbert-space": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-cauchy-schwarz-in-an-inner-product-space": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-bounded-linear-operator": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-compact-linear-operator": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-self-adjoint-positive-unitary-and-normal-operator": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-double-orthogonal-complement-is-closure": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Assume Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]) and use $Q$, $H_N$, $a$, and $W$ from\n[[lem-qft-coercive-polynomial-form-domain]]. For each $f\\in H_N$, let $Rf$ be\nthe unique vector of $Q$ satisfying\n$$a(v,Rf)=\\langle v,f\\rangle_{H_N}\\qquad(v\\in Q),$$\nand view $R$ as an operator $H_N\\to H_N$ by the inclusion of\n[[lem-qft-cube-poincare-compact-form-inclusion]]. Then $R$ is bounded, compact,\nself-adjoint, positive, injective, and has dense range; moreover\n$\\|R\\|\\le\\tfrac12$. This is the variational inverse of the shifted polynomial\nform. It establishes compactness and dense range but does not itself assert a\ndifferential-operator realization.",
        "strategy": "**Proof technique:** direct.\n\n1.1 For fixed $f\\in H_N$, the functional $v\\mapsto\\langle v,f\\rangle_{H_N}$ is linear and satisfies $|\\langle v,f\\rangle|\\le\\|v\\|_2\\|f\\|_2\\le\\|v\\|_Q\\|f\\|_2$ by [F1] and Cauchy–Schwarz. Thus [F3] applied to the Hilbert space $(Q,a)$ gives a unique $Rf\\in Q$ with the stated variational identity. For $\\alpha,\\beta\\in\\mathbb C$, conjugate-linearity in the second argument gives $a(v,\\alpha Rf+\\beta Rg)=\\langle v,\\alpha f+\\beta g\\rangle$, so uniqueness makes $R$ linear. [A1, F1, F3, F4, given]\n\n2.1 Taking $v=Rf$ in the identity gives $\\|Rf\\|_Q^2=a(Rf,Rf)=\\langle Rf,f\\rangle$. Hence $\\|Rf\\|_Q^2\\le\\|Rf\\|_2\\|f\\|_2\\le\\|Rf\\|_Q\\|f\\|_2$, so $\\|Rf\\|_Q\\le\\|f\\|_2$ (with the zero case immediate). Consequently $R:H_N\\to H_N$ is bounded by [F5]. [step 1.1, F1, F4, F5, algebra]\n\n2.2 The same identity and $a(Rf,Rf)\\ge2\\|Rf\\|_2^2$ give $2\\|Rf\\|_2^2\\le\\|Rf\\|_2\\|f\\|_2$; division when $Rf\\ne0$ yields $\\|R\\|\\le\\tfrac12$. If $Rf=0$, the variational identity says $\\langle v,f\\rangle=0$ for every $v\\in Q$; since $Q$ is dense in $H_N$ by [F1], continuity of the inner product gives $\\langle f,f\\rangle=0$, hence $f=0$. Thus $R$ is injective. [step 1.1, F1, F4, algebra]\n\n2.3 For $f,g\\in H_N$, the variational identity and conjugate symmetry of $a$ yield $\\langle Rf,g\\rangle=a(Rf,Rg)=\\langle f,Rg\\rangle$, so $R$ is self-adjoint by [F7]. Also $\\langle Rf,f\\rangle=a(Rf,Rf)\\ge0$, so it is positive. [step 1.1, F1, F7, algebra]\n\n3.1 The map $f\\mapsto Rf$ is bounded from $H_N$ into $Q$ by step 2.1, and the inclusion $J:Q\\to H_N$ is compact by [F2]. Therefore for every bounded $B\\subseteq H_N$, $R(B)=J(R(B))$ has compact closure, which is exactly compactness by [F6]. [step 2.1, F2, F6]\n\n4.1 If $z\\perp\\operatorname{ran}R$, then $\\langle Rf,z\\rangle=0$ for every $f\\in H_N$. Self-adjointness from step 2.3 gives $\\langle f,Rz\\rangle=0$ for every $f$, so $Rz=0$ and step 2.2 gives $z=0$. Thus $(\\operatorname{ran}R)^\\perp=\\{0\\}$, and [F8] gives $\\overline{\\operatorname{ran}R}=(\\operatorname{ran}R)^{\\perp\\perp}=\\{0\\}^\\perp=H_N$. Therefore $R$ has dense range. [step 2.2, step 2.3, F8] ∎",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Gerald Teschl, Mathematical Methods in Quantum Mechanics, 2nd ed., §2.3, pp.76–83",
