        "proof_strategy": "direct"
      },
      {
        "id": "thm-qft-self-adjoint-regulated-interaction",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Self-adjoint unbounded polynomial interaction at fixed regulator",
        "reference": "items/thm-qft-self-adjoint-regulated-interaction.md#Statement",
        "design_state": "Authored draft with complete local argument; independent review remains pending.",
        "dependency_level": 4,
        "deps": [
          "def-qft-regulated-polynomial-hamiltonian",
          "lem-qft-coercive-polynomial-form-domain",
          "lem-qft-polynomial-positive-compact-resolvent",
          "thm-spectral-theorem-for-compact-self-adjoint-operators",
          "thm-hilbert-space-fourier-expansion",
          "cor-finite-dimensional-inner-product-spaces-have-orthonormal-bases",
          "thm-cauchy-schwarz-in-an-inner-product-space",
          "def-unbounded-linear-operator-domain-and-graph",
          "def-adjoint-of-a-densely-defined-unbounded-operator",
          "def-symmetric-self-adjoint-and-essentially-self-adjoint",
          "def-countable-choice"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-qft-regulated-polynomial-hamiltonian": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-qft-coercive-polynomial-form-domain": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-qft-polynomial-positive-compact-resolvent": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-spectral-theorem-for-compact-self-adjoint-operators": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-hilbert-space-fourier-expansion": "mathematical-premise",
          "cor-finite-dimensional-inner-product-spaces-have-orthonormal-bases": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-cauchy-schwarz-in-an-inner-product-space": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-unbounded-linear-operator-domain-and-graph": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-adjoint-of-a-densely-defined-unbounded-operator": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-symmetric-self-adjoint-and-essentially-self-adjoint": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Assume Countable Choice and the data of\n[[lem-qft-polynomial-positive-compact-resolvent]]. Its variational operator $R$\nis compact, self-adjoint, positive, and injective on the infinite-dimensional\ncomplex Hilbert space $H_N$. Let $(\\mu_n)_{n\\ge1}$ be its positive eigenvalues\nin nonincreasing order, with multiplicity, and choose corresponding\northonormal eigenvectors $(\\psi_n)_{n\\ge1}$. Put\n$E_n:=\\mu_n^{-1}-1$ and define\n$$D(S):=\\left\\{u=\\sum_{n\\ge1}c_n\\psi_n\\in H_N:\\sum_{n\\ge1}E_n^2|c_n|^2<\\infty\\right\\},\\qquad Su:=\\sum_{n\\ge1}E_nc_n\\psi_n.$$\nThe series for $u$ converges in $H_N$ by the Hilbert-space expansion theorem.\nThen $S$ is self-adjoint and satisfies $\\langle Su,u\\rangle\\ge\\|u\\|_2^2$\nfor every $u\\in D(S)$; also $(S+I)^{-1}=R$ is compact. For every\n$u\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^N;\\mathbb C)$,\n$$Su=-\\sum_{j=1}^Na_j\\partial_j^2u+Wu.$$\nConsequently $h:=S-(C_0+1)I$, on the same domain, is a self-adjoint realization\nof the differential expression in\n[[def-qft-regulated-polynomial-hamiltonian]] and satisfies\n$\\langle hu,u\\rangle\\ge-C_0\\|u\\|_2^2$ for every $u\\in D(h)$.\nIt has compact resolvent, meaning that for some $z\\in\\mathbb C$ the map\n$h-zI:D(h)\\to H_N$ is bijective with bounded inverse on $H_N$, and that\ninverse is compact; here $z=-(C_0+2)$ and $(h+(C_0+2)I)^{-1}=R$. Put\n$\\lambda_n:=E_n-C_0-1$, the eigenvalues of $h$; equivalently,\n$$D(h)=\\left\\{u=\\sum_nc_n\\psi_n:\\sum_n\\lambda_n^2|c_n|^2<\\infty\\right\\}.$$\nFor every $u\\in Q$,\n$$a(u,u)=\\sum_{n\\ge1}(E_n+1)|\\langle u,\\psi_n\\rangle|^2,\\qquad q_h(u):=a(u,u)-(C_0+2)\\|u\\|_2^2 =\\sum_{n\\ge1}\\lambda_n|\\langle u,\\psi_n\\rangle|^2.$$\nThe lowest eigenvalue is attained and has finite multiplicity. This selects a\nform realization; it does not assert that the differential expression on\n$C_c^\\infty$ is essentially self-adjoint or that it has no other self-adjoint\nextensions.",
        "strategy": "**Proof technique:** direct.\n\n1.1 By [F1], $R$ is compact and self-adjoint. Applying [F2] and using $\\ker R=\\{0\\}$ shows that the closed span of its nonzero eigenspaces is all of $H_N$. If $R\\psi=\\mu\\psi$ with $\\psi\\ne0$, then $\\mu\\|\\psi\\|^2=\\langle R\\psi,\\psi\\rangle\\ge0$ by positivity, while injectivity rules out $\\mu=0$; hence every nonzero eigenvalue is positive. [F1, F2, algebra]\n\n1.2 The domains defined by the squared coefficient sums for $E_n$ and $\\lambda_n=E_n-(C_0+1)$ coincide. Indeed\n$$\\lambda_n^2\\le2E_n^2+2(C_0+1)^2,\\qquad E_n^2\\le2\\lambda_n^2+2(C_0+1)^2.$$\nMultiplying by $|c_n|^2$ and summing preserves finiteness in both directions because $\\sum_n|c_n|^2=\\|u\\|_2^2<\\infty$ by [F4]. This proves the displayed coefficient-domain description of $D(h)$. [F4, algebra]\n\n2.1 Each eigenspace is finite-dimensional by [F2], and so has an orthonormal basis by [F3]. The set of nonzero eigenvalues is at most countable, and [A1] selects these finite bases; their union is an orthonormal family whose closed span is $H_N$ by step 1.1. The space is infinite-dimensional: the indicators of the pairwise disjoint unit cubes $[r,r+1)\\times[0,1)^{N-1}$, $r\\in\\mathbb N$, are nonzero mutually orthogonal $L^2$ vectors. Thus the family can be indexed by $n\\ge1$. The bound $\\|R\\|\\le\\tfrac12$ gives $0<\\mu_n\\le\\tfrac12$ and $E_n\\ge1$. The compact spectral theorem says only finitely many eigenvalues satisfy $\\mu\\ge\\varepsilon$ for each $\\varepsilon>0$; recursively taking a largest remaining eigenvalue (which exists because above half its positive supremum there are only finitely many) orders them nonincreasingly with multiplicity and gives $\\mu_n\\to0$, hence $E_n\\to\\infty$. [A1, F1, F2, F3, step 1.1, algebra]\n\n3.1 The family $(\\psi_n)$ is complete by step 1.1, so [F4] gives $u=\\sum_n\\langle u,\\psi_n\\rangle\\psi_n$ in norm for every $u\\in H_N$ and Parseval. In particular $D(S)$ is dense because it contains all finite linear combinations of the $\\psi_n$. Since each $E_n$ is real, the diagonal formula for $S$ gives $\\langle Su,v\\rangle=\\langle u,Sv\\rangle$ for finite sums, and then for all $u,v\\in D(S)$ by [F7] and convergence of the coefficient series; thus $S$ is symmetric. For $u\\in D(S)$ with coefficients $c_n$, Cauchy--Schwarz [F7] makes $\\sum_nE_n|c_n|^2$ absolutely convergent, and step 2.1 gives $E_n\\ge1$, so $\\langle Su,u\\rangle=\\sum_nE_n|c_n|^2\\ge\\sum_n|c_n|^2=\\|u\\|_2^2$. [F4, F7, step 1.1, step 2.1, algebra]\n\n4.1 To compute the adjoint domain, let $v=\\sum_nd_n\\psi_n\\in D(S^*)$ and write $S^*v=z=\\sum_nz_n\\psi_n$. Testing the adjoint identity on each $\\psi_n$ gives $z_n=E_nd_n$. Because $z\\in H_N$, Parseval gives $\\sum_nE_n^2|d_n|^2<\\infty$, so $v\\in D(S)$. Conversely, if this sum is finite, then $z:=\\sum_nE_nd_n\\psi_n\\in H_N$ and the coefficient identity $\\langle Su,v\\rangle=\\langle u,z\\rangle$ holds first for finite sums and then for every $u\\in D(S)$ by [F7]; hence $v\\in D(S^*)$ and $S^*v=Sv$. Therefore $D(S^*)=D(S)$ and $S^*=S$ as operators; by the unbounded self-adjointness definition in [F5], $S$ is self-adjoint. [F4, F5, F7, step 3.1, algebra]\n\n4.2 On each $\\psi_n$, $(S+I)^{-1}\\psi_n=(E_n+1)^{-1}\\psi_n=\\mu_n\\psi_n=R\\psi_n$. Both sides are bounded operators on $H_N$ and agree on the span of the complete family, so they agree on all of $H_N$. Thus $(S+I)^{-1}=R$, which is compact by [F1]. [F1, F2, step 2.1, step 3.1]\n\n5.1 The variational identity for $R$, applied to $f=\\psi_n$, gives $a(v,R\\psi_n)=\\langle v,\\psi_n\\rangle$ for every $v\\in Q$. Since $R\\psi_n=\\mu_n\\psi_n$ and $\\mu_n>0$, each $\\psi_n$ lies in $Q$ and\n$$a(v,\\psi_n)=\\mu_n^{-1}\\langle v,\\psi_n\\rangle.$$\nThus $\\xi_n:=\\sqrt{\\mu_n}\\,\\psi_n$ is an orthonormal family in $Q$. If $v\\in Q$ is orthogonal in $a$ to every $\\xi_n$, the displayed identity makes all $H_N$ coefficients $\\langle v,\\psi_n\\rangle$ zero. The $H_N$ completeness of $(\\psi_n)$ and injectivity of $Q\\hookrightarrow H_N$ force $v=0$ in $Q$. Hence $(\\xi_n)$ is complete in $Q$, and Parseval in $(Q,a)$ gives\n$$a(v,v)=\\sum_n\\mu_n^{-1}|\\langle v,\\psi_n\\rangle|^2=\\sum_n(E_n+1)|\\langle v,\\psi_n\\rangle|^2.$$\n[F1, F4, F6, step 1.1, step 2.1, step 4.2, algebra]\n\n5.2 Let $u\\in C_c^\\infty$ and set $f:=(-\\sum_ja_j\\partial_j^2+W+1)u\\in H_N$. Integration by parts gives $a(u,v)=\\langle f,v\\rangle$ for $v\\in C_c^\\infty$; both sides are continuous in $v$ for the $Q$ norm by [F7], and $C_c^\\infty$ is dense in $Q$, so the identity holds for all $v\\in Q$. It is the defining variational identity for $Rf$, and uniqueness in [F1] gives $Rf=u$. Hence $u\\in\\operatorname{ran}R=D(S)$ and $(S+I)u=f$, so $Su=-\\sum_ja_j\\partial_j^2u+Wu$. [F1, F7, step 4.2, algebra]\n\n6.1 Parseval in $H_N$ gives $\\|v\\|_2^2=\\sum_n|\\langle v,\\psi_n\\rangle|^2$. Subtracting $(C_0+2)$ times this identity from step 5.1 yields the claimed formula for $q_h(v)$, because $E_n+1-(C_0+2)=E_n-C_0-1=\\lambda_n$. [step 5.1, F4, algebra]\n\n6.2 If $u\\in D(h)=D(S)$ and $c_n=\\langle u,\\psi_n\\rangle$, then $\\sum_nE_n^2|c_n|^2<\\infty$ by the definition of $D(S)$. Since $E_n\\ge1$, $E_n+1\\le2E_n^2$, so $\\sum_n(E_n+1)|c_n|^2<\\infty$. Step 5.1 therefore gives $u\\in Q$. Thus every eigenvector, and indeed every operator-domain vector, belongs to the form domain. [F2, step 5.1, algebra]\n\n7.1 Subtracting the real scalar $(C_0+1)I$ preserves self-adjointness on the same domain, and $E_n\\ge1$ gives eigenvalues $\\lambda_n=E_n-C_0-1\\ge-C_0$. For $u\\in D(h)$, the diagonal expansion and Cauchy--Schwarz [F7] make $\\langle hu,u\\rangle=\\sum_n\\lambda_n|\\langle u,\\psi_n\\rangle|^2$ absolutely convergent; Parseval then gives $\\langle hu,u\\rangle\\ge-C_0\\|u\\|_2^2$. The lowest eigenvalue corresponds to the largest eigenvalue $\\mu_1$ of $R$; its eigenspace is nonzero and finite-dimensional by [F2]. Since $W=V+C_0+1$, step 5.2 shows that $h=S-(C_0+1)I$ extends the specified polynomial differential expression. Finally $h+(C_0+2)I=S+I$, whose inverse is the compact operator $R$ by step 4.2. These statements establish the declared realization and all listed scope limits. [F1, F2, F7, step 2.1, step 4.2, step 5.2] ∎",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Gerald Teschl, Mathematical Methods in Quantum Mechanics, 2nd ed., §2.3, pp.76–83",
              "url": "https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~gerald/ftp/book-schroe/schroe2.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "Richard S. Laugesen, Spectral Theory of Partial Differential Equations — Lecture Notes, Chapter 4, pp.27–32",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1203.2344v1"
            }
          ]
        },
        "library": "mathematics",
        "proof_strategy": "direct"
      },
      {
        "id": "thm-qft-regulated-global-unitary-evolution",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Global unitary evolution at fixed finite regulator",
        "reference": "items/thm-qft-regulated-global-unitary-evolution.md#Statement",
        "design_state": "Authored draft with complete local argument; independent review remains pending.",
        "dependency_level": 5,
        "deps": [
          "thm-qft-self-adjoint-regulated-interaction",
          "thm-hilbert-space-fourier-expansion",
          "thm-cauchy-schwarz-in-an-inner-product-space",
          "def-countable-choice"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "thm-qft-self-adjoint-regulated-interaction": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-hilbert-space-fourier-expansion": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-cauchy-schwarz-in-an-inner-product-space": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Assume Countable Choice and let $h$ be the self-adjoint operator of\n[[thm-qft-self-adjoint-regulated-interaction]], with complete orthonormal\neigenfamily $(\\psi_n)_{n\\ge1}$ and real eigenvalues\n$\\lambda_n=E_n-C_0-1$. For $t\\in\\mathbb R$ define\n$$U(t)f:=\\sum_{n\\ge1}e^{-it\\lambda_n}\\langle f,\\psi_n\\rangle\\psi_n \\qquad(f\\in H_N).$$\nThen the series converges in $H_N$ for every $f$; $U(t)$ is unitary,\n$U(0)=I$, $U(s+t)=U(s)U(t)$, and $t\\mapsto U(t)f$ is norm-continuous for\nevery $f$. Its norm derivative at any $t$ exists exactly for $f\\in D(h)$, and\nthen\n$$\\frac{d}{dt}U(t)f=-i\\,hU(t)f.$$\nThis is a mathematical one-parameter group with a dimensionless real\nparameter; it makes no physical unit or empirical claim.",
        "strategy": "**Proof technique:** direct.\n\n**Given:** $f\\in H_N$, its coefficients $c_n=\\langle f,\\psi_n\\rangle$, and the real sequence $(\\lambda_n)$.\n\n1.1 Parseval gives $\\sum_n|c_n|^2=\\|f\\|^2$. Since $|e^{-it\\lambda_n}|=1$, the finite partial sums defining $U(t)f$ are Cauchy in $H_N$, so converge; Parseval gives $\\|U(t)f\\|^2=\\sum_n|c_n|^2=\\|f\\|^2$. The coefficient formula shows $U(-t)U(t)f=f=U(t)U(-t)f$ and $U(s)U(t)f=U(s+t)f$, first on finite sums and then on all of $H_N$ by norm convergence and the norm bound. Thus $U(t)$ is a surjective isometry, hence unitary, and $U(0)=I$. [A1, F1, F2, algebra]\n\n1.2 Fix $s\\in\\mathbb R$ and $f\\in H_N$. Given $\\varepsilon>0$, choose a finite set $F\\subset\\mathbb N$ with $\\sum_{n\\notin F}|c_n|^2<\\varepsilon^2/64$, possible by convergence of the nonnegative series in [F2]. The norm of the difference on the complement is at most $2(\\sum_{n\\notin F}|c_n|^2)^{1/2}<\\varepsilon/4$. On the finite set $F$, each scalar $e^{-it\\lambda_n}$ is continuous in $t$, so the finite partial difference has norm below $3\\varepsilon/4$ for $t$ sufficiently close to $s$. The triangle inequality proves $\\|U(t)f-U(s)f\\|<\\varepsilon$, establishing strong continuity. [F2, algebra]\n\n1.3 Let $f\\in D(h)$, so $\\sum_n\\lambda_n^2|c_n|^2<\\infty$. Since $|e^{-it\\lambda_n}|=1$, $U(t)f$ has the same finite weighted coefficient sum and belongs to $D(h)$. For $r\\ne0$,\n$$\\frac{U(t+r)f-U(t)f}{r} =\\sum_ne^{-it\\lambda_n}\\frac{e^{-ir\\lambda_n}-1}{r}c_n\\psi_n.$$\nThe scalar integral identity $e^{-ir\\lambda}-1=\\int_0^r(-i\\lambda)e^{-is\\lambda}\\,ds$ gives $|(e^{-ir\\lambda}-1)/r|\\le|\\lambda|$. Thus the difference quotient coefficients are dominated in square sum by $\\lambda_n^2|c_n|^2$. On each finite set they converge to $-i\\lambda_ne^{-it\\lambda_n}c_n$; choosing a finite set with small tail in that dominating series and then taking $r\\to0$ proves convergence in $H_N$ to $-i\\sum_n\\lambda_ne^{-it\\lambda_n}c_n\\psi_n=-ihU(t)f$. [F1, F2, algebra]\n\n2.1 Conversely, suppose the norm derivative at $t=0$ exists and equals $g\\in H_N$. Continuity of the coefficient functional $v\\mapsto\\langle v,\\psi_n\\rangle$ from [F3] makes its value on the difference quotient converge to $-i\\lambda_nc_n$. Hence $\\langle g,\\psi_n\\rangle=-i\\lambda_nc_n$ for every $n$. Parseval for $g$ implies $\\sum_n\\lambda_n^2|c_n|^2=\\|g\\|^2<\\infty$, so $f\\in D(h)$ by [F1]. If the derivative exists at any other $t$, the same coefficient argument multiplies each coefficient by a unit phase and yields the identical summability condition. Thus the differentiability domain is exactly $D(h)$ at every $t$. [F1, F2, F3, algebra] ∎",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Gerald Teschl, Mathematical Methods in Quantum Mechanics, 2nd ed., §5.1, pp.145–149",
              "url": "https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~gerald/ftp/book-schroe/schroe2.pdf",
              "locator": "§5.1, printed pp.145–149: unitary groups from self-adjoint operators and their generator domains."
            },
            {
              "title": "Richard S. Laugesen, Spectral Theory of Partial Differential Equations — Lecture Notes, Chapter 4, pp.27–32",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1203.2344v1",
              "locator": "Chapter 4, printed pp.27–32: discrete spectral expansions from compact resolvent."
            }
          ]
        },
        "library": "mathematics",
        "proof_strategy": "direct"
      },
      {
        "id": "thm-qft-regulated-gibbs-trace",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Finite-regulator Gibbs trace and bounded expectations",
        "reference": "items/thm-qft-regulated-gibbs-trace.md#Statement",
        "design_state": "Authored draft with complete local argument; independent review remains pending.",
        "dependency_level": 5,
        "deps": [
          "def-qft-regulated-polynomial-hamiltonian",
          "thm-qft-self-adjoint-regulated-interaction",
          "lem-qft-hermite-oscillator-research-supplier",
          "lem-qft-coercive-polynomial-form-domain",
          "thm-hilbert-space-fourier-expansion",
          "thm-riesz-representation-for-hilbert-space",
          "thm-cauchy-schwarz-in-an-inner-product-space",
          "lem-finite-rank-operators-are-compact",
          "thm-norm-limit-of-compact-operators-is-compact",
          "lem-nuclear-series-characterizes-trace-norm",
          "thm-trace-of-a-positive-operator-is-the-sum-of-its-eigenvalues",
          "thm-trace-is-absolutely-convergent-and-basis-independent",
          "def-bounded-linear-operator",
          "def-countable-choice"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-qft-regulated-polynomial-hamiltonian": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-qft-self-adjoint-regulated-interaction": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-qft-hermite-oscillator-research-supplier": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-qft-coercive-polynomial-form-domain": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-hilbert-space-fourier-expansion": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-riesz-representation-for-hilbert-space": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-cauchy-schwarz-in-an-inner-product-space": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-finite-rank-operators-are-compact": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-norm-limit-of-compact-operators-is-compact": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-nuclear-series-characterizes-trace-norm": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-trace-of-a-positive-operator-is-the-sum-of-its-eigenvalues": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-trace-is-absolutely-convergent-and-basis-independent": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-bounded-linear-operator": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Assume Countable Choice and use the finite-mode data, form domain $Q$, and\nself-adjoint $h$ from [[thm-qft-self-adjoint-regulated-interaction]]. Let\n$\\lambda_0\\le\\lambda_1\\le\\cdots$ be its eigenvalues repeated with\nmultiplicity, and let $\\psi_k$ be a corresponding orthonormal eigenbasis. For\n$\\beta>0$ define the finite-rank truncations\n$$\\rho_{\\beta,M}f:=\\sum_{k=0}^M e^{-\\beta\\lambda_k}\\langle f,\\psi_k\\rangle\\psi_k.$$\nThe proof establishes their operator-norm limit $\\rho_\\beta$, with action\n$$\\rho_\\beta f:=\\sum_{k\\ge0}e^{-\\beta\\lambda_k}\\langle f,\\psi_k\\rangle\\psi_k.$$\nPut $\\alpha_j:=\\sqrt{a_jc}$ and let the oscillator eigenvalues\n$\\epsilon_k$ be the nondecreasing ordering, with multiplicity, of\n$$\\epsilon_{\\mathbf n}=\\sum_{j=1}^N(2n_j+1)\\alpha_j, \\qquad\\mathbf n\\in\\mathbb N_0^N.$$\nThen $\\rho_\\beta$ is positive trace class and its trace\n$$Z_\\beta:=\\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho_\\beta)=\\sum_{k\\ge0}e^{-\\beta\\lambda_k}$$\nsatisfies the finite bound\n$$0<Z_\\beta\\le e^{\\beta C_0} \\prod_{j=1}^N\\frac{e^{-\\beta\\alpha_j}}{1-e^{-2\\beta\\alpha_j}}<\\infty.$$\nFor every bounded linear operator $B:H_N\\to H_N$, the normalized trace\nexpectation is well defined and\n$$Z_\\beta^{-1}\\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho_\\beta B) =Z_\\beta^{-1}\\sum_{k\\ge0}e^{-\\beta\\lambda_k} \\langle B\\psi_k,\\psi_k\\rangle,$$\nwith absolute convergence. This is a finite-$N$ mathematical trace result;\nit assumes no statistical data and makes no continuum-limit claim.",
        "strategy": "**Proof technique:** direct.\n\n**Given:** $N\\ge1$, $a_j,c>0$, $V(q)\\ge c|q|^2-C_0$, the interacting form, and $\\beta>0$.\n\n1.1 Each $\\epsilon_{\\mathbf n}$ is finite, and only finitely many multi-indices have $\\epsilon_{\\mathbf n}\\le M$ for any real $M$: for $M\\ge0$, that inequality bounds every nonnegative integer $n_j$ by $M/(2\\alpha_j)$, while for $M<0$ there are none. Fix the explicit degree-then-lexicographic enumeration of $\\mathbb N_0^N$. Recursively, among the remaining tuples choose one of least energy, breaking a tie by that fixed enumeration; such a least energy exists because below the energy of the first remaining tuple there are only finitely many candidates. Every tuple is eventually listed, since only finitely many tuples have energy at most its energy. This gives the nondecreasing list $(\\epsilon_k)_{k\\ge0}$ with multiplicity. [F2, algebra]\n\n1.2 On $C_c^\\infty$, the shifted interacting form has weight $V+C_0+2\\ge c|q|^2+2$, so it dominates the shifted oscillator form from [F2]. Therefore a Cauchy sequence in $Q$ is Cauchy in the oscillator form norm; the identity map extends to a continuous map $Q\\to Q_{\\mathrm{osc}}$, with the same $L^2$ representative because both completions inject into $H_N$ by [F3]. Subtracting the shifts in the two form norms gives\n$$q_h(u)=a(u,u)-(C_0+2)\\|u\\|_2^2\\ge q_{\\mathrm{osc}}(u)-C_0\\|u\\|_2^2\\qquad(u\\in Q).$$\n[F2, F3, algebra]\n\n2.1 Fix $k\\ge0$ and let $L_k=\\operatorname{span}\\{\\psi_0,\\ldots,\\psi_k\\}$. The linear map from $L_k$ to the span of the first $k$ oscillator eigenvectors, sending $u$ to its $k$ oscillator coefficients, has a nonzero kernel because $\\dim L_k=k+1>k$. Choose $u\\ne0$ in that kernel. Since $u$ lies in the span of the first $k+1$ interacting eigenvectors, [F1] gives $q_h(u)\\le\\lambda_k\\|u\\|_2^2$. Its oscillator coefficients at indices below $k$ vanish, so [F2] gives $q_{\\mathrm{osc}}(u)\\ge\\epsilon_k\\|u\\|_2^2$. Step 1.2 now yields $\\lambda_k\\|u\\|_2^2\\ge q_h(u)\\ge(\\epsilon_k-C_0)\\|u\\|_2^2$, hence\n$$\\lambda_k\\ge\\epsilon_k-C_0.$$\nThis argument also covers $k=0$, when the span of zero oscillator vectors is $\\{0\\}$. [A1, F1, F2, step 1.2, algebra]\n\n3.1 By step 2.1 and $\\beta>0$,\n$$\\sum_{k\\ge0}e^{-\\beta\\lambda_k} \\le e^{\\beta C_0}\\sum_{\\mathbf n\\in\\mathbb N_0^N} e^{-\\beta\\epsilon_{\\mathbf n}} =e^{\\beta C_0}\\prod_{j=1}^N \\left(\\sum_{n_j\\ge0}e^{-\\beta(2n_j+1)\\alpha_j}\\right) =e^{\\beta C_0}\\prod_{j=1}^N \\frac{e^{-\\beta\\alpha_j}}{1-e^{-2\\beta\\alpha_j}}.$$\nThe factorization follows first for finite rectangular sums by distributivity and then by increasing limits of nonnegative sums. Each denominator is positive because $0<e^{-2\\beta\\alpha_j}<1$. The lower bound $Z_\\beta>0$ follows from its positive ground term. [F2, step 2.1, algebra]\n\n4.1 The coefficients satisfy $0<e^{-\\beta\\lambda_k}\\le e^{\\beta C_0}$ by [F1]. For each $f$, Parseval makes the finite partial sums in the defining series for $\\rho_\\beta f$ Cauchy in $H_N$, so the series converges; its coefficient formula gives linearity and $\\|\\rho_\\beta f\\|_2\\le e^{\\beta C_0}\\|f\\|_2$. Moreover Parseval gives\n$$\\|\\rho_\\beta-\\rho_{\\beta,M}\\|\\le\\sup_{k>M}e^{-\\beta\\lambda_k}\\longrightarrow0,$$\nsince $\\lambda_k\\to+\\infty$ and $\\beta>0$. Each truncation is finite rank and hence compact by [F10], so the operator-norm limit $\\rho_\\beta$ is compact by [F11]. The same convergence makes its rank-one partial sums converge in operator norm, and their coefficient sum is $\\sum_ke^{-\\beta\\lambda_k}=Z_\\beta<\\infty$; thus $\\rho_\\beta$ is trace class by [F4]. [A1, F1, F2, F4, F10, F11, F12, step 3.1, algebra]\n\n5.1 Since each $e^{-\\beta\\lambda_k}$ is real, the Fourier expansion [F12] gives\n$$\\langle\\rho_\\beta f,g\\rangle=\\sum_k e^{-\\beta\\lambda_k}\\langle f,\\psi_k\\rangle\\langle\\psi_k,g\\rangle=\\langle f,\\rho_\\beta g\\rangle$$\nfor all $f,g\\in H_N$; the series is absolutely convergent by Cauchy--Schwarz\nfor the coefficient sequences and boundedness of the weights. Thus\n$\\rho_\\beta$ is self-adjoint. It is positive because\n$$\\langle\\rho_\\beta f,f\\rangle =\\sum_ke^{-\\beta\\lambda_k}|\\langle f,\\psi_k\\rangle|^2\\ge0.$$\nThe defining series gives $\\rho_\\beta\\psi_k=e^{-\\beta\\lambda_k}\\psi_k$. If $\\rho_\\beta x=\\mu x$, taking Fourier coefficients shows $(e^{-\\beta\\lambda_k}-\\mu)\\langle x,\\psi_k\\rangle=0$ for every $k$. Completeness [F1, F12] then implies that every nonzero eigenspace is exactly the span of basis vectors with the corresponding repeated diagonal value; since every diagonal value is positive, $0$ is not an eigenvalue. Thus the eigenvalues with multiplicity are precisely $(e^{-\\beta\\lambda_k})$, and [F5] gives $\\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho_\\beta)=Z_\\beta$. For bounded $B$, each $\\rho_{\\beta,M}B$ is finite rank and hence compact by [F10], while\n$$\\|\\rho_{\\beta,M}B-\\rho_\\beta B\\|\\le\\|\\rho_{\\beta,M}-\\rho_\\beta\\|\\,\\|B\\|\\to0$$\nby step 4.1. Therefore $\\rho_\\beta B$ is compact by [F11]. The functional $x\\mapsto\\langle Bx,\\psi_k\\rangle$ is linear and has norm at most $\\|B\\|$ by [F9] and [F8]. By [F6] it is represented by a vector $w_k$ with $\\|w_k\\|\\le\\|B\\|$ and $\\langle Bx,\\psi_k\\rangle=\\langle x,w_k\\rangle$. Hence\n$$\\rho_\\beta Bx=\\sum_k\\langle x,w_k\\rangle e^{-\\beta\\lambda_k}\\psi_k.$$\nThe partial sums converge in operator norm: the norm of the tail after $M$ is at most $\\|B\\|\\sum_{k>M}e^{-\\beta\\lambda_k}\\to0$ by step 3.1. This nuclear representation has coefficient sum at most $\\|B\\|Z_\\beta$. Since $\\rho_\\beta B$ is compact as shown above, [F4] gives that it is trace class. Its basis trace in $(\\psi_k)$ is absolutely convergent and equals the trace by [F7]. The $k$th diagonal entry is\n$$\\langle \\rho_\\beta B\\psi_k,\\psi_k\\rangle =e^{-\\beta\\lambda_k}\\langle B\\psi_k,\\psi_k\\rangle,$$\nso this basis sum is the asserted numerator. Each term is bounded in modulus by $e^{-\\beta\\lambda_k}\\|B\\|$ by [F8] and [F9], giving the stated absolute bound. Division by $Z_\\beta>0$ proves the expectation formula. [A1, F1, F4, F5, F6, F7, F8, F9, F10, F11, F12, step 3.1, step 4.1, algebra] ∎",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Gerald Teschl, Mathematical Methods in Quantum Mechanics, 2nd ed., §§2.3 and 6.3, pp.76–83 and 163–169",
              "url": "https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~gerald/ftp/book-schroe/schroe2.pdf",
              "locator": "§2.3, form realization; §6.3, trace ideals and eigenvalue sums for positive operators."
            },
            {
              "title": "Richard S. Laugesen, Spectral Theory of Partial Differential Equations — Lecture Notes, Chapters 8–9, pp.48–54",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1203.2344v1",
              "locator": "Chapter 8, confining-well compactness; Chapter 9, variational comparison of eigenvalues."
            }
          ]
        },
        "library": "mathematics",
        "proof_strategy": "direct"
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-qft-regulated-ground-variational-bound",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Ground eigenvalue as the form variational infimum",
        "reference": "items/lem-qft-regulated-ground-variational-bound.md#Statement",
        "design_state": "Authored draft with complete local argument; independent review remains pending.",
        "dependency_level": 5,
        "deps": [
          "thm-qft-self-adjoint-regulated-interaction",
          "thm-hilbert-space-fourier-expansion",
          "def-countable-choice"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "thm-qft-self-adjoint-regulated-interaction": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-hilbert-space-fourier-expansion": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Assume Countable Choice and use $Q$, $q_h$, and the operator $h$ from\n[[thm-qft-self-adjoint-regulated-interaction]]. Let $\\lambda_1$ be the least\neigenvalue of $h$. Then\n$$\\inf\\{q_h(u):u\\in Q,\\ \\|u\\|_2=1\\}=\\lambda_1.$$\nThe infimum is attained by every normalized vector in the lowest eigenspace;\nno simplicity or nondegeneracy is assumed.",
        "strategy": "**Proof technique:** direct.\n\n**Given:** $u\\in Q$ and the ordered real eigenvalue list.\n\n1.1 Since $\\lambda_n\\ge\\lambda_1$ for every $n$, [A1], [F1] and [F2] imply\n$$q_h(u)-\\lambda_1\\|u\\|_2^2 =\\sum_n(\\lambda_n-\\lambda_1)|\\langle u,\\psi_n\\rangle|^2\\ge0.$$\nThus every unit vector in $Q$ has form value at least $\\lambda_1$. [A1, F1, F2, algebra]\n\n2.1 A normalized eigenvector $\\psi_1$ belongs to $D(h)\\subseteq Q$ by\n[[thm-qft-self-adjoint-regulated-interaction]], has norm one, and its sole\nnonzero coefficient is at an eigenvalue $\\lambda_1$. Therefore\n$q_h(\\psi_1)=\\lambda_1$, so the lower bound is attained and equals the\ninfimum. If the lowest eigenspace has dimension greater than one, the same\ncoefficient calculation applies to every normalized vector in it. [F1, F2, step 1.1, algebra] ∎",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Gerald Teschl, Mathematical Methods in Quantum Mechanics, 2nd ed., §4.4, pp.139–141",
              "url": "https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~gerald/ftp/book-schroe/schroe2.pdf",
              "locator": "§4.4, printed pp.139–141: Rayleigh quotients and variational eigenvalue characterizations."
            },
            {
              "title": "Richard S. Laugesen, Spectral Theory of Partial Differential Equations — Lecture Notes, Chapter 9, pp.51–54",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1203.2344v1",
              "locator": "Chapter 9, printed pp.51–54: Rayleigh quotient and variational eigenvalue bounds."
            }
          ]
        },
        "library": "mathematics",
        "proof_strategy": "direct"
      }
    ]
  },
